;; Docs for std/simpletest.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "simpletest.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Assertions that print a line each, with-test for a body under a scope, and a fake filesystem.")
  (see expect with-test with-fake-fs)
  (example """
    (import (std prelude))
    (import (std simpletest))

    (: main (-> (Vec string) int))
    (defun (main args)
      (expect "adds" (+ 1 2) 3)              ; prints: ok: adds
      (with-test "reads the fixture"
        (with-fake-fs (("a.txt" "hello"))
          (expect "read it" (call-with-input-file "a.txt" read-all) (Ok "hello"))))
      0)
    """)
  (reference """
    @strong{Assertions.} @code{expect}, @code{expect-true} and @code{fail}
    print one line for each assertion. A passed assertion prints
    @code{ok: label}. A failed assertion prints a line that starts with
    @code{FAILURE:}, and sets the exit code of the program to 1, even when
    @code{main} answers 0. Thus a test file is a command that a shell or CI
    can check, and the compiler's own test runner looks for the
    @code{FAILURE:} lines. A failed assertion does not stop the program.

    @strong{(std simpletest) and (std test).} This module has no registry
    and no runner: a test file is an ordinary program that calls the
    assertions. @code{(std test)} is the newer harness. Its tests are silent
    unless they fail, they are registered with @code{deftest}, and
    @code{bjo test} runs them. Each @code{deftest} runs in a scope as
    @code{with-test} does. The fake filesystem of this module works in
    both.

    @strong{with-test} runs a body under a deadline, and fails if the body
    leaves a resource open. The scope gives four things. A test gets a
    timeout, so a leaked fiber is a failure that names the test, and the
    suite does not hang. The scope waits for the fibers that the body
    spawned, so an assertion inside one is not missed. The resources that
    the body opened are released. And several failures in one body are all
    reported, because an assertion does not stop the body.

    @strong{The fake filesystem.} @code{with-fake-fs} installs a handler for
    each operation of the @code{FS} effect of the prelude. Everything that
    goes to the disk through it answers from a map instead: the text and
    byte openers, @code{file-read-text} and its neighbours,
    @code{file-exists?}, @code{file-delete}, @code{file-move} and the
    directory functions. See @code{FakeFs} for its rules.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Assertions
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc expect
  (summary "Asserts that two values are equal, and prints the result.")
  (arg label "What the assertion is about. The printed line shows it.")
  (arg actual "The value to test.")
  (arg wanted "The value it must be equal to.")
  (see expect-true fail)
  (example """
    (expect "parse" (parse "12") (Ok 12))
    ;; prints:  ok: parse
    ;; or:      FAILURE: parse gave (Ok 13) but wanted (Ok 12)
    """)
  (reference """
    A failure also sets the exit code of the program to 1.
    """))

(:doc expect-true
  (summary "Asserts that a condition is #t, and prints the result.")
  (arg label "What the assertion is about. The printed line shows it.")
  (arg ok "The condition.")
  (see expect fail)
  (reference """
    Prints @code{ok: label}, or @code{FAILURE: label} and sets the exit
    code of the program to 1.
    """))

(:doc fail
  (summary "Fails at once: for a branch that a test must not reach.")
  (arg label "Why the branch is wrong.")
  (see expect expect-true)
  (reference """
    Prints @code{FAILURE: label} and sets the exit code of the program to
    1. It does not stop the program.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; A test body, under a scope
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc with-test
  (summary "Runs a body under a deadline, and fails if it left a resource open.")
  (form (with-test name body ...))
  (form (with-test name #:within d body ...))
  (form (with-test name #:open-ok body ...))
  (form (with-test name #:within d #:open-ok body ...))
  (arg name "A string: the name of the test. The failure lines show it.")
  (arg d "The deadline, a Duration. @code{(default-test-deadline)}, 5 seconds, when it is not given.")
  (rest body "The forms of the test. At least one form. Their values are dropped.")
  (see default-test-deadline with-fake-fs expect)
  (example """
    (with-test "reads the fixture"
      (with-fake-fs (("a.txt" "hello"))
        (expect "read it" (call-with-input-file "a.txt" read-all) (Ok "hello"))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The body runs under @code{with-deadline}, so it is a scope. The scope
    waits for the fibers that the body spawned, and releases the resources
    that the body opened. When both options are given, @code{#:within}
    comes first.

    The test fails, with a @code{FAILURE:} line that names it:

    - when the body leaves a resource open. The check is done before the
      scope closes, because the scope releases the resources when it closes,
      and a test must fail if it does not close its files. @code{#:open-ok}
      turns off the check. Use it in a test of what the closing of a scope
      does.
    - when the deadline ends the scope. A fiber that the body leaked is
      cancelled by the deadline, and the scope then closes cleanly, so
      without this check the test would pass, slowly.
    - when the body raises an exception, a cancellation included. The
      failure shows the message.

    It catches every @code{System.Exception}, a cancellation included.
    Usually, a cancellation must not be caught. But this is the top of a
    test, and a failure that names the test is better than a stack trace.
    """))

(:doc default-test-deadline
  (summary "The deadline of a with-test that gives none: 5 seconds.")
  (returns "(seconds 5).")
  (see with-test)
  (reference """
    Exported because the template of @code{with-test} names it.
    """))

(:doc overran?
  (summary "Whether a deadline ended a scope.")
  (arg holder "A box that holds the scope, or @code{None} when the scope did not start.")
  (returns "#t when the scope was cancelled because of its deadline, else #f.")
  (see with-test)
  (reference """
    A fiber that a body leaked is cancelled by the deadline, and a
    cancelled child is not a failure, so the scope closes cleanly. The token
    of the scope keeps the reason for the cancellation, so
    @code{with-test} keeps the scope in a box and reads the reason after
    the scope has closed. Exported because the template of
    @code{with-test} names it.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; A filesystem that is not one
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc FakeFs
  (summary "A filesystem in memory, for with-fake-fs-of.")
  (see fake-fs with-fake-fs with-fake-fs-of fake-fs-written fake-fs-contents)
  (example """
    (def fs (fake-fs (list (Tuple "in.txt" "a b c"))))
    (with-fake-fs-of fs
      (ignore (file-write-text "out.txt" "done")))
    (fake-fs-written fs "out.txt")   ; (Some "done")
    """)
  (reference """
    It is mutable, so that a later read sees a write.

    @strong{Paths.} A seeded path is a file, and every directory above a
    file exists. A path written in the fake can be read after. Paths are
    compared as text after one normalization: a backslash becomes a slash,
    and a leading @code{./} and a trailing @code{/} are removed. @code{""}
    and @code{"."} are the root. Nothing resolves @code{..}, and a relative
    path is not made absolute, so @code{"a.txt"} and @code{"/tmp/a.txt"}
    are different files.

    @strong{Contents.} A write to a path replaces what the path held. A
    write in @code{Append} mode continues what the path held. Bytes are kept
    as they were written, also when they are not UTF-8. A read sees what
    has reached the stream of a writer, also when the writer is still open.

    @strong{Errors.} An operation that fails answers the exception type that
    the .NET call would raise, so a test that matches on the error matches
    either way.

    @strong{Time.} The fake has a clock of its own, so that a test about
    modified times does not depend on how fast it runs. The clock starts at
    2000-01-01T00:00:00Z, which is the time of every seed. Each write that
    is opened and each directory that is created moves the clock one second
    forward and stamps the path with it. Thus a file written later is always
    newer. A move keeps the times of what it moves, as a rename on a disk
    does. @code{fake-fs-set-modified!} sets a time.

    @strong{Ports.} The openers answer a @code{MemoryStream}, and the ports
    on it are made by the same code that makes ports on a file. Thus a fake
    port is registered on the scope that opened it, and a leak test on a
    fake filesystem is the same as a leak test on a real one.
    """))

(:doc fake-fs
  (summary "A new fake filesystem, seeded with files.")
  (arg seeds "Pairs of a path and the text of the file at that path.")
  (returns "The filesystem. Every directory above a seeded file exists.")
  (see with-fake-fs-of with-fake-fs)
  (example """
    (fake-fs (list (Tuple "notes.txt" "a b c")
                   (Tuple "dir/empty.txt" "")))
    """))

(:doc fake-fs-written
  (summary "What was written to a path of a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (arg path "The path.")
  (returns "The text written to the path, decoded as UTF-8. @code{None} when nothing was written to it, also when it is a seeded file.")
  (see fake-fs-contents with-fake-fs-of)
  (reference """
    It works after the port is closed, because it reads the
    @code{MemoryStream} with @code{ToArray}.
    """))

(:doc fake-fs-contents
  (summary "What a file of a fake filesystem holds, whether it was seeded or written.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (arg path "The path.")
  (returns "The text of the file, decoded as UTF-8. @code{None} when there is no file at the path.")
  (see fake-fs-written))

(:doc fake-fs-set-modified!
  (summary "Sets the modified time of a path of a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (arg path "The path.")
  (arg ms "The time, in Unix milliseconds.")
  (see FakeFs)
  (example """
    ;; for a test such as "skip what was written in the last five seconds"
    (fake-fs-set-modified! fs "old.txt" 946684800000L)
    """))

;; --- The handlers --------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc fake-fs-reader
  (summary "The handler of open-read-stream for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function from a path to a stream on what the file holds. A directory
    answers @code{System.UnauthorizedAccessException}. A missing file
    answers @code{System.IO.FileNotFoundException}, or
    @code{System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException} when its directory is
    missing too.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of)
  (reference """
    The handlers are exported because the templates of
    @code{with-fake-fs-of} name them. Use @code{with-fake-fs-of} to install
    them.
    """))

(:doc fake-fs-writer
  (summary "The handler of open-write-stream for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function from a path and a @code{WriteMode} to a stream to write the
    file through. @code{Append} starts the stream with what the path held.
    @code{CreateNew} on a file that exists answers
    @code{System.IO.IOException}. A directory answers
    @code{System.UnauthorizedAccessException}, and a missing directory above
    the path answers @code{System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException}. Each
    write that opens moves the clock one second forward and stamps the path.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-info
  (summary "The handler of fs-info for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function from a path to its @code{FsInfo}, or @code{None} when nothing
    is at the path. The size is in bytes, and is 0 for a directory. The
    modified time is the stamp of the path, or the start of the clock.
    Nothing is a link.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-list
  (summary "The handler of fs-list for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function from a directory to the paths of what is directly in it,
    sorted. The paths are whole, as @code{Directory.GetFileSystemEntries}
    gives them: @code{"dir/a.txt"}, not @code{"a.txt"}. A path that is not a
    directory answers @code{System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException}.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-create-directory
  (summary "The handler of fs-create-directory for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function that creates a directory and each missing directory above
    it, as @code{Directory.CreateDirectory} does. A directory that exists
    is not an error. A file at the path answers
    @code{System.IO.IOException}. A new directory moves the clock one second
    forward and is stamped with it.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-delete-file
  (summary "The handler of fs-delete-file for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function that deletes a file. A missing file is not an error, as with
    @code{File.Delete}. A directory answers
    @code{System.UnauthorizedAccessException}.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-delete-directory
  (summary "The handler of fs-delete-directory for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function that deletes an empty directory. A directory that is not
    empty, the root, and a file at the path answer
    @code{System.IO.IOException}. A missing directory answers
    @code{System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException}.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

(:doc fake-fs-move
  (summary "The handler of fs-move for a fake filesystem.")
  (arg fs "The filesystem.")
  (returns """
    A function that moves a file, or a directory and everything below it,
    with the modified times. The source is checked first, as
    @code{File.Move} does: a missing source answers
    @code{System.IO.FileNotFoundException}. A destination that exists
    answers @code{System.IO.IOException}, unless the mode is
    @code{MoveReplace} and a file replaces a file. A missing directory above
    the destination answers @code{System.IO.DirectoryNotFoundException}.
    """)
  (see with-fake-fs-of))

;; --- The forms -----------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc with-fake-fs-of
  (summary "Runs a body with an existing fake filesystem installed for the FS effect.")
  (form (with-fake-fs-of fs body ...))
  (arg fs "A @code{FakeFs}. It is evaluated once.")
  (rest body "What to run. At least one form. Its last value is the value of the form.")
  (see with-fake-fs fake-fs fake-fs-written)
  (example """
    (def fs (fake-fs (list)))
    (with-fake-fs-of fs
      (ignore (file-write-text "out.txt" "done")))
    (expect "wrote it" (fake-fs-written fs "out.txt") (Some "done"))
    """)
  (reference """
    Use it, not @code{with-fake-fs}, to examine the filesystem after the
    body.

    It is a handler frame, not a scope: it does not wait for a fiber spawned
    in the body. The fiber keeps the fake, because a spawn takes the
    environment as it was at the spawn. But the assertions of the body, and
    the leak check of @code{with-test}, run before the fiber is done. So put
    a fiber that reads or writes the fake in a @code{with-scope} of its own,
    inside this form. This form opens no scope, because a scope would hide
    from @code{with-test} what the body left open.

    A macro that writes a @code{with-handler} must give the operation names
    through @code{inject}, as this one does. @code{with-handler} derives the
    cell it binds from the name it is given, and a renamed name derives a
    cell that nothing declares.
    """))

(:doc with-fake-fs
  (summary "Runs a body with a new fake filesystem, seeded with files, installed for the FS effect.")
  (form (with-fake-fs ((path contents) ...) body ...))
  (arg path "The path of a seeded file.")
  (arg contents "The text of that file.")
  (rest body "What to run. At least one form. Its last value is the value of the form.")
  (see with-fake-fs-of FakeFs with-test)
  (example """
    (with-fake-fs (("notes.txt" "a b c")
                   ("empty.txt" ""))
      (expect "read it back" (call-with-input-file "notes.txt" read-all) (Ok "a b c")))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{(with-fake-fs-of (fake-fs seeds) body ...)}. Use
    @code{with-fake-fs-of} to examine the filesystem after the body. A
    fiber spawned in the body is not waited for: see
    @code{with-fake-fs-of}.
    """))
